The Patient AK Case Study: From Static Report to Living Care Plan
What We Found in the Genomic Data
When we uploaded Patient AK's Foundation Medicine report to CrisPRO, the agents immediately identified something the standard interpretation missed:

Mutation 1: MBD4 c.1239delA (homozygous frameshift)

Function: MBD4 is part of the base excision repair (BER) pathway

Status: Complete loss of function (both copies knocked out)

Implication: Her cancer cells can't fix small DNA errors

Mutation 2: TP53 R175H (detected by IHC)

Function: TP53 is the "guardian of the genome"—detects DNA damage and triggers cell death

Status: Mutant, dominant-negative (actively broken, not just absent)

Implication: Her cancer cells can't sense DNA damage or trigger apoptosis

The Synthetic Lethality Insight (Module 14)
Most reports would note these mutations individually. But CrisPRO's Synthetic Lethality Agent—powered by the Evo2 foundation model (7B parameters)—connected the dots:

Step 1: Gene Essentiality Scoring

